import{_ as o,o as t,c as d,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"A WorkBuddy connector for Rho","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/workbuddy-connector-episode.md","filePath":"records/workbuddy-connector-episode.md"}'),a={name:"records/workbuddy-connector-episode.md"};function s(n,e,c,h,i,l){return t(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="a-workbuddy-connector-for-rho" tabindex="-1">A WorkBuddy connector for Rho <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-workbuddy-connector-for-rho" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A WorkBuddy connector for Rho&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Updated 2026-11-10. After the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/outside-agent-episode">outside-Agent episode</a> the user asked whether WorkBuddy can set up Rho&#39;s experts, skills and connectors by itself, and then asked for one package made for WorkBuddy, to submit at <a href="https://open.workbuddy.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://open.workbuddy.cn/</a>. This episode reads what WorkBuddy had done with Rho, how WorkBuddy 5.9.3 loads each of the three, runs WorkBuddy&#39;s own agent core on the user&#39;s requests, and builds the package: a CLI connector, <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/blob/main/docs/../adapters/workbuddy-rho/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">adapters/workbuddy-rho</a>.</p><h2 id="what-workbuddy-had-done" tabindex="-1">What WorkBuddy had done <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-workbuddy-had-done" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What WorkBuddy had done&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From WorkBuddy&#39;s own session files and its folder <code>~/.workbuddy</code> on this Mac:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What happened</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Connector</td><td>Its Agent added Rho&#39;s MCP server to <code>~/.workbuddy/mcp.json</code> three times (2026-11-06 and 11-07): <code>rho-project</code> for the airway project, <code>rho-ggtree</code>, <code>rho-pa14-quorum-sensing</code></td><td>None ran. WorkBuddy runs a server in that file only after the person approves it under its connectors: <code>mcp-approvals.json</code>, keyed by a hash of the command, arguments, environment names and folder, was empty. A server an Agent writes there is shown as disabled, and a new grant changes the arguments, so the hash. In the 11-07 session the Agent found no <code>rho_*</code> tools, and its connection request returned &quot;skipped&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Expert</td><td>Asked with <code>@skill:expert-manager 帮我创建一个 Rho使用专家…</code> (11-09), WorkBuddy&#39;s built-in expert skill made <code>rho-data-analyst</code> in 494 s: persona, a copied Rho Skill, an avatar; validated, registered, packaged</td><td>Its Skill is the one before <code>rho open</code> and <code>rho tool</code>, and teaches <code>rho connect</code> and an MCP server. On 11-10 the user&#39;s CSV request to that expert again had no Rho tools; the Agent wrote its own MCP client script and was interrupted at 289 s</td></tr><tr><td>Skill</td><td>WorkBuddy installs skills from its market (<code>marketplace-skill-installer</code>), which has no Rho</td><td><code>~/.workbuddy/skills/rho</code> was imported by the user, at 10:42 UTC on 11-09: seven minutes after the airway conversation began, so that conversation could not list it</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two statements of the outside-Agent episode change with this: <code>rho-project</code> is the airway project&#39;s server, not another project&#39;s, and the Skill was not ignored but not yet installed.</p><h2 id="workbuddy-s-agent-on-the-request" tabindex="-1">WorkBuddy&#39;s agent on the request <a class="header-anchor" href="#workbuddy-s-agent-on-the-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;WorkBuddy&#39;s agent on the request&quot;">​</a></h2><p>WorkBuddy&#39;s agent core is a CodeBuddy CLI inside the app (<code>app.asar.unpacked/cli/bin/codebuddy</code>). Run with <code>ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE</code> and a throwaway config folder it has WorkBuddy&#39;s built-in skills. Its own models need the desktop app&#39;s login, so a custom model pointing at the local gateway carried <code>westlakehpc/glm-5.3-flash</code>; the tools the desktop app adds (market search, connector requests, image generation) are absent there.</p><p>Asked &quot;帮我在 WorkBuddy 里配置好 Rho 相关的专家、技能和连接器，之后我要在这个项目里用 Rho 做数据分析。&quot; on a copy of the airway project, three runs (83–169 s) went to the market skills, <code>recommend-experts</code>, <code>recommend-connectors</code> and <code>marketplace-skill-installer</code>, found no market tools and stopped. None made an expert locally or ran <code>rho</code>; one asked whether &quot;Rho&quot; meant RhoA/ROCK or Spearman&#39;s rho. Those runs could not write files or run commands (an allow-list problem of the harness, fixed below), so they show where WorkBuddy looks, not what it does next.</p><p>So WorkBuddy does not reach Rho by itself: an expert is made only when named, a connector it writes does not run, and a skill comes from the market. A market connector is what WorkBuddy offers a person and installs itself.</p><h2 id="the-package" tabindex="-1">The package <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-package" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The package&quot;">​</a></h2><p>WorkBuddy&#39;s open platform takes a connector as a folder with <code>connector-meta.json</code>, either <code>mcp.json</code> or <code>cli.json</code>, an icon and optional <code>skills/</code>. Its <code>mcp.json</code> must hold one server whose arguments cannot carry a placeholder or a per-machine path, and Rho&#39;s MCP server needs the path of a project&#39;s grant file. <code>rho tool</code> reaches the same four tools from a shell, in the conversation, with no approval and no new conversation. Of the 312 connectors in WorkBuddy&#39;s local market cache, 46 are listed as CLI connectors, most installed by <code>npm install -g</code> with a Node WorkBuddy provides.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>connector-meta.json</code></td><td><code>source</code> <code>rho-workspace</code>, <code>type</code> <code>cli</code>, version 1.0.0, Chinese and English names, descriptions and three examples each, WorkBuddy 4.24.0 or later</td></tr><tr><td><code>cli.json</code></td><td><code>init</code>: <code>npm install -g @rho-workspace/rho@latest</code> with Node <code>&gt;=22.22.0 &lt;27</code> (Rho&#39;s <code>engines</code>); <code>versionCheck</code>: <code>rho --version</code> at least 0.5.8; <code>status</code>: <code>rho --version</code> matching a version, since Rho has no sign-in; <code>unAuth</code>: the same harmless command; no <code>auth</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>icon.svg</code></td><td>Rho&#39;s ρ icon, as in the Harness plugin</td></tr><tr><td><code>skills/rho/SKILL.md</code></td><td>Open with <code>rho open</code>, tell the person Rho is a page in their browser, work through <code>rho tool … --as workbuddy</code>, leave <code>mcp.json</code> alone, run R and Python as Notebook cells, report the page&#39;s address. The build adds Rho&#39;s seven references beside it</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>node adapters/workbuddy-rho/build.mjs</code> checks the fields WorkBuddy&#39;s page requires, that the runtime range is Rho&#39;s <code>engines.node</code>, that this Rho is at least <code>minVersion</code>, that <code>statusMatch</code> matches <code>rho --version</code>, that each link reaches a packaged file and that no file names the building machine&#39;s home or holds a credential, and writes <code>work/workbuddy-rho/rho-workspace/</code> and <code>rho-workspace-1.0.0.zip</code>. <code>test/workbuddy-connector.test.mjs</code> runs the build and the three <code>cli.json</code> commands against this build. Rho&#39;s own <code>connect</code> reference now also tells an Agent in WorkBuddy to use <code>rho tool</code> and leave <code>mcp.json</code> alone.</p><h2 id="trials-with-workbuddy-s-agent" tabindex="-1">Trials with WorkBuddy&#39;s agent <a class="header-anchor" href="#trials-with-workbuddy-s-agent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trials with WorkBuddy&#39;s agent&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>scripts/outside-agent-trials.mjs --client workbuddy</code> runs the outside-Agent cases with WorkBuddy&#39;s agent core instead of Claude Code: a throwaway home and WorkBuddy config folder, its built-in skills, the tools allowed in its settings, the gateway route as a custom model. <code>bare</code> has <code>rho</code> on PATH (as after the connector&#39;s <code>init</code>, without its Skill); <code>skill</code> also has the connector&#39;s Skill in the config folder&#39;s <code>skills/</code>. Three runs each, in parallel.</p><p><strong>&quot;使用rho 启动该项目&quot;</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Seconds</th><th>Calls</th><th>Folder a project</th><th>Told it is a browser page, with the address</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bare</td><td>35, 35, 34</td><td>6, 5, 5: <code>ls</code>, <code>.rho</code>, <code>rho --help</code>, <code>rho open</code></td><td>3 of 3</td><td>3 of 3</td></tr><tr><td>skill</td><td>26, 25, 26</td><td>2: the Skill, <code>rho open</code></td><td>3 of 3</td><td>3 of 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p>WorkBuddy&#39;s shell rebuilds <code>PATH</code> with <code>/usr/bin</code> first, so the harness&#39;s recorder for <code>open</code> was passed over and these six runs opened up to twelve real tabs, to their own temporary services, on the user&#39;s screen. The <code>rho</code> wrapper of the harness now puts the recorder first itself.</p><p><strong>&quot;Draw the volcano plot in the notebook; I will select the genes I care about.&quot;</strong>, with the folder opened first:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Seconds</th><th>Result</th><th>Address in the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bare</td><td>168, 171</td><td>A Notebook cell through <code>rho tool</code>; points of 17,173 marks bound to rows</td><td>1 of 2</td></tr><tr><td>bare</td><td>235</td><td>No Rho command: &quot;项目里没有 Jupyter notebook&quot;, a separate plotly HTML through <code>Rscript</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>skill</td><td>97, 94, 168</td><td>A Notebook cell through <code>rho tool</code> in each; 17,173, 17,173 and 17,165 bound marks</td><td>3 of 3</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The second <code>skill</code> run did not load the Skill and went to <code>rho tool</code> from <code>.rho/README.md</code>. With the Skill no run read Rho&#39;s state files more than once or edited an MCP configuration; the slow one spent 40 s finding <code>rho tool index</code>&#39;s paging and input forms.</p><h2 id="not-done" tabindex="-1">Not done <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-done" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not done&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Submitted:</strong> nothing. The platform has no upload API; the user submits the zip, and the connector works only once Rho 0.5.8 (with <code>rho open</code> and <code>rho tool</code>) is on npm.</li><li><strong>WorkBuddy&#39;s own model and desktop app:</strong> the trials used WorkBuddy&#39;s agent core with GLM-5.3-flash, without the app&#39;s host tools or its model. Installing through the connector, its <code>versionCheck</code> and <code>status</code>, and how the app shows a CLI connector without sign-in are unobserved until the connector is installed from the market.</li><li><strong>Platform rules:</strong> whether review accepts a CLI connector with no <code>auth</code>, and the zip&#39;s expected root, are unknown; the README gives what to tell the reviewer.</li><li><strong>Windows</strong> through WorkBuddy is unobserved (<code>rho.cmd</code> runs in CI).</li><li>The user&#39;s own <code>rho-data-analyst</code> expert and <code>~/.workbuddy/skills/rho</code> keep the older Skill; the user&#39;s <code>mcp.json</code> keeps three servers that never ran. Nothing in the user&#39;s WorkBuddy folder was changed.</li></ul>',25)])])}const k=o(a,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,k as default};
